# XCU jobs
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# jobs is intrinsic (XCU 1.7). The standard speaks of background jobs of a
# job-control shell, so each test turns job control on with set -m (which
# the User Portability Option provides for any shell) and starts sleep
# processes with &. A test that cannot turn job control on reports
# UNRESOLVED. The tests keep to what the page says for a shell that is
# not a subshell and is non-interactive only where jobs makes no
# interactive condition. Started processes are always killed and reaped.

assertion  XCU.jobs.001
anchor     utilities/jobs.html#STDOUT
anchor     utilities/jobs.html#DESCRIPTION
class      conditional
when       UP
since      1992
says       jobs lists a running background job as one line made of [number],
           one space, + (the only job is the current job), one space,
           Running, one space, and the command.
test       t/001-running.sh

assertion  XCU.jobs.002
anchor     utilities/jobs.html#OPTIONS
anchor     utilities/jobs.html#STDOUT
class      conditional
when       UP
since      1992
says       jobs -p writes the process ID of the process group leader of the
           job, one per line; for a job made from a single command that is
           the ID the shell reports in $!.
test       t/002-p.sh

assertion  XCU.jobs.003
anchor     utilities/jobs.html#OPTIONS
anchor     utilities/jobs.html#STDOUT
class      conditional
when       UP
since      1992
says       jobs -l puts the process group ID between the current marker and
           the state, so the first line has the fields [number], +, the ID,
           Running and the command.
test       t/003-l.sh

assertion  XCU.jobs.004
anchor     utilities/jobs.html#STDOUT
class      conditional
when       UP
since      1992
says       A background job that has ended with status zero is reported once
           as Done (with the same line layout) and is then removed from
           the list.
test       t/004-done.sh

assertion  XCU.jobs.005
anchor     utilities/jobs.html#STDOUT
class      conditional
when       UP
since      1992
says       A background job that has ended with a non-zero status n is
           reported as Done(n).
test       t/005-done-code.sh

assertion  XCU.jobs.006
anchor     utilities/jobs.html#STDOUT
class      conditional
when       UP
since      1992
says       A background job stopped by SIGTSTP is reported with the state
           Stopped (or Suspended, with or without the signal name in
           parentheses).
test       t/006-stopped.sh

assertion  XCU.jobs.007
anchor     utilities/jobs.html#STDOUT
class      conditional
when       UP
since      1992
says       When a suspended job exists the current job, marked +, is a
           suspended one even if a later job is running.
test       t/007-current-suspended.sh

assertion  XCU.jobs.008
anchor     utilities/jobs.html#OPERANDS
class      conditional
when       UP
since      1992
says       With a job_id operand (%?string, a job whose command contains the
           string) jobs reports only that job.
test       t/008-job-id.sh

assertion  XCU.jobs.009
anchor     utilities/jobs.html#EXIT STATUS
anchor     utilities/jobs.html#STDERR
class      conditional
when       UP
since      1992
says       jobs with a job_id that names no job exits greater than zero and
           writes nothing to standard output.
test       t/009-no-such-job.sh

assertion  XCU.jobs.010
anchor     utilities/jobs.html#EXIT STATUS
anchor     utilities/jobs.html#STDOUT
class      conditional
when       UP
since      1992
says       With no background job jobs writes nothing and exits zero.
test       t/010-empty.sh
